4275	///      linked cells on a page, and with a one-page memory about one
4276	///      page in nine shrank the hyperlink map after a light page and
4277	///      grew it again, copying the whole page, on the next heavy one.
4278	///      Remembering three pages makes that about one page in ninety.
4279	///      If `last` and the page before it used none of a dimension,
4280	///      only the page before `last` counts, so output that stops using
4281	///      a dimension gets standard pages again as quickly as before.
4282	///
4283	/// For example, suppose a program draws a few hundred unique styles on
4284	/// every screen. The first page grows its style capacity a few times.
4285	/// Every page after that starts with the larger capacity and never needs
4286	/// to grow. Once the output goes back to plain text, two full pages of
4287	/// plain text are enough for new pages to return to the standard size.
4288	///
4289	/// A page with the standard capacity comes from the memory pool. When
4290	/// the carried capacity is too large for a pool item, this gives the
4291	/// page fewer rows so that it still fits, as long as that keeps at least
4292	/// 90% of the rows per byte that a heap page with all its rows would
4293	/// have. Every page has a block of storage for styles, graphemes,
4294	/// strings, and hyperlinks in addition to its rows, so fewer rows in the
4295	/// same memory means less scrollback per byte. For example, if the heap
4296	/// page would be 1.5 times the size of a pool item and hold 215 rows,
4297	/// the pooled page must hold at least 215 / 1.5 * 0.9 = 129 rows.
4298	///
